module preconcentrationDetection

manifest NafionResin
manifest PhosphateBuffer
manifest BetaPhycoerythrin_Sample

// ============================================================
// Multiplexed Proteomic Sample Preconcentration Device Using
// Surface-Patterned Ion-Selective Membrane.
// Lee, Song & Han
// Lab Chip, 2008, 8(4): 596-601
// DOI: 10.1039/b717220f
//
// Platform: PDMS microfluidic chip (20 mm x 25 mm) with
// surface-patterned planar Nafion ion-selective membrane
// embedded at the PDMS/glass interface by plasma bonding.
// Microchannels: 50 um deep x 50 um wide (all channels).
// Channel layout (dual gate design): one central sample
// channel flanked symmetrically by two buffer channels;
// Nafion membrane stripe printed on glass substrate connects
// all three channels at the channel bottom as a permselective
// junction (analogous to nanochannels in prior silicon device).
//
// Separation physics: electrokinetic trapping.
// Phase 1 - Depletion mode: voltage difference (Vdiff = V1-V2)
//   applied across sample and buffer channels through Nafion
//   membrane (V1=50V, V2=50V, buffer GND). Normal field (En)
//   generates a depletion region in the sample channel; charged
//   molecules are pushed away from the membrane toward V1.
// Phase 2 - Concentration mode: V2 reduced to 25V while V1
//   held at 50V. Tangential field (Et) drives electroosmotic
//   flow (EOF) along sample channel toward the depletion zone.
//   Molecules are stacked in front of the depletion boundary.
//   Balance of En and Et produces a stable concentrated plug.
// Optimum Vdiff = 25V (V1=50V, V2=25V); above 25V depletion
// zone destabilized by secondary nonlinear electrokinetics.
//
// Model protein: beta-Phycoerythrin (beta-PE; MW 240 kDa,
//   pI 4.3; Sigma-Aldrich). Dissolved in 10^-2 M phosphate
//   buffer pH 7. Concentrations tested: 40 pM, 400 pM, 4 nM,
//   40 nM. Internal reference standards: 2, 4, 8 uM.
// Performance: concentration factor ~10^4 in 5 min (400 pM
//   starting -> 8 uM reference in 5 min); ~5x10^4 in 10 min
//   (40 pM starting); concentrated plug volume ~200 pL;
//   coefficient of variance 11.5% across 3 devices.
//
// Nafion membrane:
//   Material  : Nafion perfluorinated ion-exchange resin
//               (Sigma-Aldrich, 5 wt% in aliphatic alcohols/H2O,
//               15-20% water content)
//   Patterning: Micro-flow patterning (preferred; more
//               reproducible thickness than micro-stamping):
//               (1) 1 uL Nafion resin filled by capillary force
//               into reversibly bonded PDMS channel (120 um
//               deep x 200 um wide); (2) liquid resin flushed
//               out by negative pressure through syringe tubing,
//               leaving thin residual layer on hydrophilic glass;
//               (3) cure at 95 C for 10 min; (4) plasma bond
//               PDMS chip to patterned glass substrate.
//   Thickness : Target 191-400 nm; >400 nm causes PDMS sealing
//               failure and leakage at membrane edges.
//               Micro-stamping alternative: plasma-treat PDMS
//               stamp, ink with Nafion, transfer to clean glass,
//               cure 95 C 10 min; thickness 0.5-1 um (less
//               reproducible; risk of leakage).
//   Bonding   : O2 plasma bonding, PDMS chip on top of Nafion-
//               patterned glass; post-bond bake 70 C for 12 h
//               to enhance bonding strength.
//
// Array format: 10 single preconcentrators arranged radially
//   with a common collection reservoir at center. Samples S1-S10
//   each at V1=50V; common reservoir at V2=25V; individual
//   ground reservoirs per buffer channel. Nafion patterned in
//   half-circle shape per unit. Concentrated volume scales
//   linearly with number of units: single ~200 pL; 10-unit
//   array ~2 nL (3500x increase vs. prior silicon device).
//   NOTE ON ARRAY ENCODING: BioScript has no loop construct;
//   explicit unrolling of 10 identical channels would require
//   10 identical dispense/mix/heat/detect blocks. This file
//   encodes the single-preconcentrator canonical unit, which
//   is the repeating element of the array. Array behavior is
//   documented in the JSON/TXT companion files.
//
// NOTE ON VOLTAGE ABSTRACTION: BioScript has no electrical
//   construct (no `voltage`, `electrode`, or `field` keyword).
//   Electrokinetic voltage application steps (depletion and
//   concentration modes) are abstracted as `heat` statements:
//   - Depletion mode (V1=50V, V2=50V, GND): heat at 50c
//     models the first voltage phase; 50c is a placeholder
//     encoding the V1 voltage level (50V) as a temperature
//     token, consistent with conventions in this library for
//     representing electrode-driven operations without a
//     native BioScript electrical primitive.
//   - Concentration mode (V1=50V, V2=25V, GND): heat at 25c
//     models the second voltage phase with the reduced V2=25V
//     as the temperature token, reflecting the voltage
//     differential that drives the electroosmotic flow.
//   Both phases operate on the same physical droplet/channel
//   contents; unique intermediate variable names are used for
//   each phase to satisfy the no-reassignment constraint.
//
// NOTE ON DURATION TOKENS: BioScript supports s and m only.
//   All durations in m or s; no h tokens used.
//   Nafion cure    : 10 min   = 10m
//   Post-bond bake : 12 h     = 720m
//   Depletion mode : 1 min    = 1m  (per paper Fig. 5 V sweep)
//   Concentration  : 5 min    = 5m  (optimum; 10^4 factor)
//   Detection      : 5 min    = 5m  (fluorescence acquisition)
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main              -- Nafion membrane patterning (cure),
//                            post-bond bake, buffer channel
//                            loading, sample loading, depletion
//                            mode, concentration mode, detection
//   BB2  concenPass        -- concenSignal > 0: concentrated plug
//                            detected above reference threshold
//                            (>=8 uM equivalent fluorescence);
//                            dispose all channels
//   BB3  concenFail        -- concenSignal <= 0: no plug detected
//                            (membrane leak, voltage failure, or
//                            insufficient preconcentration time);
//                            drain all channels
//   BB4  finalCleanup      -- terminal: dispose QC signal, end
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: Nafion membrane patterning - micro-flow patterning.
//   1 uL Nafion resin placed at reservoir of reversibly bonded
//   PDMS channel (120 um deep x 200 um wide); capillary force
//   fills channel immediately. Liquid resin flushed out by
//   negative pressure via syringe tubing on opposite end,
//   leaving a thin Nafion layer on the hydrophilic glass.
//   Cure patterned stripe at 95 C for 10 min on hotplate.
//   Target thickness: 191-400 nm.
//   NOTE: The patterning PDMS channel is temporary and
//   reversibly bonded (removed before device assembly).
//   This step is encoded as a heat operation on NafionResin
//   abstracting the flow-coat + thermal cure sequence.
//   Off-chip physical fabrication; no liquid handling on the
//   final device at this stage.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

nafionResin    = dispense 1 units of NafionResin
nafionCured    = mix nafionResin with nafionResin for 1m
heat nafionCured at 95c for 10m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: PDMS chip plasma bonding and post-bond bake.
//   PDMS chip (50 um deep x 50 um wide channels, dual gate
//   layout: 1 sample channel center + 2 buffer channels
//   flanking) plasma bonded on top of Nafion-patterned glass.
//   No microscope alignment required (non-critical alignment).
//   Post-bond bake: 70 C for 12 h (= 720 min) to enhance
//   bonding strength between PDMS and glass/Nafion.
//   Encoded as heat on cured membrane; abstracts bonding bake.
//   NOTE: Self-mix pattern used (nafionCured with itself) to
//   represent a single-material bake step with no second
//   reagent. Same pattern as warkiani_spiral_ctc_isolation.bs
//   ethanol priming step; documented compiler behavior.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

heat nafionCured at 70c for 720m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: Load buffer solution into buffer channels (flanking).
//   10^-2 M phosphate buffer pH 7 loaded into both outer
//   buffer channels (left and right) of the dual gate design.
//   Buffer channels are grounded (GND) during both depletion
//   and concentration modes. Dual buffer channels improve
//   stability of the depletion region by generating depletion
//   zones from both sides of the sample channel simultaneously.
//   Dispense two independent buffer droplets representing the
//   two flanking buffer channels.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

bufferLeft  = dispense 1 units of PhosphateBuffer
bufferRight = dispense 1 units of PhosphateBuffer
bufferDual  = mix bufferLeft with bufferRight for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Load protein sample into sample channel (center).
//   beta-Phycoerythrin (beta-PE; MW 240 kDa, pI 4.3) at
//   working concentration (e.g. 4 nM as canonical condition
//   from Fig. 6) dissolved in 10^-2 M phosphate buffer pH 7.
//   Sample loaded into central microchannel; both ends of
//   sample channel accessible for electrode connections
//   (V1 at one end, V2 at other end during concentration mode).
//   NOTE: `mix` abstracts sample loading into the microchannel
//   co-resident with the buffer; this is not a DMF droplet
//   merge but represents channel filling with protein solution.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

proteinSample   = dispense 1 units of BetaPhycoerythrin_Sample
loadedSample    = mix proteinSample with bufferDual for 30s

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: Depletion mode (Phase 1).
//   Voltage configuration: V1=50V (sample channel inlet),
//   V2=50V (sample channel outlet), buffer channels at GND.
//   A potential difference is applied across sample and buffer
//   channels through the Nafion membrane. The normal field
//   component (En) across the membrane depletes ions in the
//   sample channel and pushes charged beta-PE molecules away
//   from the membrane toward the V1 reservoir.
//   Duration: 1 min to establish and stabilize the depletion
//   region before switching to concentration mode.
//   ABSTRACTION: heat at 50c encodes V1=50V operating voltage.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

depletedSample  = mix loadedSample with bufferDual for 1m
heat depletedSample at 50c for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Concentration mode (Phase 2).
//   Voltage configuration: V1=50V, V2=25V, buffer GND.
//   Vdiff = V1-V2 = 25V (optimum; above 25V secondary
//   nonlinear electrokinetics destabilize concentrated zone).
//   Tangential field (Et) generates electroosmotic flow (EOF)
//   along sample channel that injects molecules toward the
//   stable depletion zone boundary. Molecules stack in front
//   of the depletion region and form a concentrated plug.
//   Duration: 5 min (canonical; achieves ~10^4 concentration
//   factor from 400 pM starting to 8 uM reference intensity;
//   from 40 pM, ~5x10^4 factor reached in 10 min).
//   Concentrated plug volume: ~200 pL; coefficient of
//   variance 11.5% across 3 devices.
//   ABSTRACTION: heat at 25c encodes V2=25V reduced voltage;
//   unique variable concentratedPlug used (no reassignment).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

concentratedPlug = mix depletedSample with bufferDual for 5m
heat concentratedPlug at 25c for 5m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: Fluorescence detection of concentrated plug.
//   Platform: Olympus IX71 inverted epifluorescence microscope
//   with thermoelectrically cooled CCD camera (Hamamatsu).
//   Fluorescence images taken every 30 s; analyzed with IPLab.
//   Mechanical shutter blocks UV between acquisitions to
//   minimize photobleaching. Neutral density filter prevents
//   CCD saturation (increased dynamic range).
//   Internal reference standards: 2, 4, 8 uM beta-PE.
//   Pass criterion: fluorescence intensity of concentrated plug
//   >= 8 uM reference (corresponding to ~10^4 fold increase
//   from 400 pM starting concentration at 5 min).
//   preconcentrationDetection module abstracts the fluorescence
//   intensity readout and comparison to reference standards.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

concenSignal = detect preconcentrationDetection on concentratedPlug for 5m

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: Preconcentration detection gate
// Pass (BB2): concenSignal > 0 -> concentrated plug detected
//             at or above 8 uM reference intensity threshold;
//             preconcentration successful. Dispose all.
// Fail (BB3): concenSignal <= 0 -> no detectable plug or
//             below threshold (membrane leakage, voltage
//             failure, or Nafion film >400 nm causing
//             imperfect sealing). Drain all channels.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (concenSignal > 0) {
    dispose concentratedPlug
} else {
    drain concentratedPlug
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose preconcentration QC signal. Experiment ends.
// For array format (10-unit radial array): repeat BB1 main
// block for each of S1-S10 sample channels (explicit unroll);
// all share common reservoir at V2=25V and individual ground
// reservoirs per buffer channel. Total concentrated volume
// scales to ~2 nL (10 x ~200 pL) for mass spectrometer
// interfacing. Array encoding is a candidate for a separate
// .bs file with explicit 10-unit unrolled structure.
// ============================================================

dispose concenSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
